{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers","id":1167121,"title":"Not native american Dreamspeakers?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-12-12T14:00:10-08:00","id":1167121,"title":"Not native american Dreamspeakers?","content":"Go on, give me your opinions about non-natives Dreamspeakers as in Shintoists or any other belief. I like both Akashic Brotherhood and the Dreamspeakers but honestly I can't really choose between the two, so I was thinking at creating some hybrid like Dreamspeaker which is trained by Akashic monks. What do you think about it? Would it work? What do you suggest? <br /> <br /> I am waiting for your opinions, thanks for the time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13197-skeeper0","name":"Skeeper0","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-12T15:50:09-08:00","id":1167166,"title":"","content":"Shinto is (in Revised) dominated by the Dreamspeakers instead of the Akashics, while Akashics get the Jains as their Spirit focused faction.<br /> <br /> The Dreamspeakers are (for a number of reasons, but in-game and just as written) one of the truly global Traditions. They're not the &quot;Native American&quot; Tradition, but the &quot;Animist/Shaman&quot; Tradition.<br /> <br /> Of course, there's nothing wrong with a Dual Tradition Dreamspeaker/Akashayana from Japan that's trained in both Dreamspeaker Shinto, and one of the main branches of the Akashic Philosophy as suits the character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-12T17:16:07-08:00","id":1167186,"title":"","content":"Shamanism of the type practiced by the Dreamspeakers is quite a global phenomena. I actually not even thought of them as &quot;native-americans&quot; until now. I think much more of the shamanistic traditions of Europe, Russia, several parts of Africa, and actually anywhere at some point in time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-13T00:52:42-08:00","id":1167239,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1167166#post1167166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Shinto is (in Revised) dominated by the Dreamspeakers instead of the Akashics, while Akashics get the Jains as their Spirit focused faction.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could you give me the names of the books that specifically bring about the Shinto Dreamspeakers? I have read something in one of their books but it's nothing more than a vague paragraph.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13197-skeeper0","name":"Skeeper0","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-13T00:55:08-08:00","id":1167240,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Skeeper0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1167239#post1167239\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Could you give me the names of the books that specifically bring about the Shinto Dreamspeakers? I have read something in one of their books but it's nothing more than a vague paragraph.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> There isn't a lot. Really Mage could use a &quot;how to incorporate real world traditions&quot; into the game better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-13T01:36:34-08:00","id":1167247,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Skeeper0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers#post1167121\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Go on, give me your opinions about non-natives Dreamspeakers as in Shintoists or any other belief. I like both Akashic Brotherhood and the Dreamspeakers but honestly I can't really choose between the two, so I was thinking at creating some hybrid like Dreamspeaker which is trained by Akashic monks. What do you think about it? Would it work? What do you suggest?<br /> <br /> I am waiting for your opinions, thanks for the time.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Would it work? Sure it can, there's room enough for all kinds of archetypes in this game. I'd say that a shinto priest probably has more in common with the Dreamspeakers than the Akashics since their whole schtick is about keeping favour with the many and varied Kami which inhabit the land (not the same Kami from Werewolf, mind you, these Kami are just spirits). In fact the Shinto and Bhudism have quite diametrically opposed worldviews, with Bhudists looking inwards at self improvement, and Shinto looking outwards for spiritual assistance.<br /> \u200b<br /> That being said, there's nothing which stops there being a mage which marries the two ideas together. Similarly there's nothing that says a shinto priest has to identify as a Dreamspeaker. They could be a Hermetic mage for instance, or even an Ecstatic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-13T02:20:20-08:00","id":1167251,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Skeeper0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1167239#post1167239\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Could you give me the names of the books that specifically bring about the Shinto Dreamspeakers? I have read something in one of their books but it's nothing more than a vague paragraph.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Revised Dreamspeaker Traditionbook discusses it. It's not very long, but I don't consider it terribly vague. It points out that Shintoism's beliefs marry directly with Dreamspeaker philosophy, leading to a natural pairing, the Dreamspeakers making up a &quot;surprisingly&quot; large portion of Awakened Japanese people, and even that the Kami help recruitment (including outside of Japan).<br /> <br /> The Revised Akashic Traditionbook also discuses Shintoism, though it basically goes directly down the dual method where most Japanese Akashics blend Buddhism and Shintoism. They might not go the full Dual Tradition route, but most learn the &quot;Kami Way&quot; because it's just part of supernatural life in Japan with Shintoism being as strong as it is there.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-13T11:50:48-08:00","id":1167372,"title":"","content":"My (admittedly limited) understanding of Japanese culture is that a lot of people who are religious have no real conflict with being Buddhist and still following Shinto rituals and traditions. Shinbutsu-shugo is the syncretist fusion of the two as an actual organized religion, if I understand it correctly. <br /> <br /> Ryukyuan is roughly the Okinawa equivalent to Shinto. The Ainu people of Hokkaido have their own separate animist beliefs. <br /> <br /> Most of the cultures in East Asia have their own &quot;folk religions involving ancestors and other spirits, gods and shaman. Shenism (China), Muism (Korea), Thanism (Vietnam), Sasna Phi (Thai and Laotian), Natism (Burma) and Jhakri (Nepal and northern India) are the ones I'm aware of.<br /> <br /> Tengerism is the term often used to refer to the Shamanistic beliefs of Mongolia and other parts of Central Asia and Siberia. <br /> <br /> Bonpo is Tibet's pre-Buddhist belief system which involves certain shamanic practices, but I know almost nothing about specifics of the beliefs. <br /> <br /> Most of the &quot;hill tribes&quot; in Southeast Asia (such as the Hmong) still practice animist religions. <br /> <br /> Indonesia has a couple of different traditions, many of the practitioners apparently &quot;officially&quot; identifying themselves as Hindus so as to avoid persecution by the Muslim dominated government. Java also has Kebatinan, which is apparently an amalgam of animist, Hindu, Buddhist and Sufi beliefs. <br /> <br /> Outside of East Asia and the classic &quot;big three&quot; of the Dreamspeakers (the Americas, Africa and Australia), there's a number of shamanic traditions in Europe (many of them discussed in Dead Magic 2), including the Sami of Finland and it's neighbors, and certain Celtic and Hungarian traditions. Polynesian traditional practices are also part of the Dreamspeakers, as are Bedouin fugara (desert shaman-mystics), and some strains of Pentecostal Christianity and New Age beliefs. <br /> <br /> As far as Japanese Shinto Dreamspeaker characters, probably a lot of Miko (shrine maiden) characters you've ever seen in anime or manga would work as such. (I now have this weird image in my head of an all Miko cabal made up of a Dreamspeaker, Akashic, and Choruser.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-13T17:25:50-08:00","id":1167454,"title":"","content":"There are other issues with the conceptualization of &quot;Dreamspeakers&quot; from the get go. It's not necessarily or only a description of the mystical practices of the members of the group. To a great degree, a group with a mystic praxis ending up being classified in the Dreamspeakers had a lot to with politics and perception. Namely, a whole shit ton of othering by the Big Three (Valoran/Chorus,La Salle/Hermetics, and Nightshade/Verbena) back at the beginning of the Traditions. Sure, there's a lot of animism (and some animatism) that crosses a lot of lines, but, seriously, some groups that had more in common with the fundamentals of Verbena belief than with anything animistic (like... say, pretty much anything we'd associate with folks like the Aztecs and their incredibly intricate and conceptualized blood magic) were forced to go sit with the &quot;weirdos&quot; over there, instead of at the &quot;cool kids&quot; table because they came from outside the Eurocentric mind set already in place. Several groups are noted as having just walked from the original meet and greets because of this kind of treatment (The Ngoma, etc.). <br /> <br /> Take it the other way and there are several VERY animistic groups that are NOT in the Dreamspeakers due to a coherent identity that allows them to organize politically as a group of mages (Kopa Loei, Bata'a).<br /> <br /> But, really, why would the Dreamspeakers get perceived as Native American/First Peoples/Indian in nature? From the get go, one of the two founders was an African shaman, Naioba. (Who, incidentally, was in cahoots with both the Ngoma and the Madzimbabwe who later joined the Euthanatos, that it throws into a talespin the whole idea of where to put her in terms of modern Mage groups... She's a Dreamspeaker mostly because she pretty much half made them up.... And, in its essence, this is the situation described above for the fundamental fractured, jury-rigged indentity of the Dreamspeakers from the outset. When the thing your group most has in common is &quot;We're REALLY different from each other but those guys over there group us together&quot;, it's going to be an uphill battle...)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-13T19:46:52-08:00","id":1167487,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1167454#post1167454\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But, really, why would the Dreamspeakers get perceived as Native American/First Peoples/Indian in nature? </div> </div> </div> </div>The real reason? Because back in 1993, a number of people working at White Wolf (developers, freelancers, etc.) were really into the whole &quot;Native American as Noble Savage&quot; thing that was big at the time, especially in the wake of Dances With Wolves (as well as drawing on stuff from the 60s and 70s that were retro-cool for Gen-X-ers), and some bought into Marshall Sahlin's &quot;original affluent society&quot; theories about primitive hunter gatherers. This was really heavily apparent in 1st ed Werewolf stuff, and carried over into 1st and 2nd ed Mage, with the Dreamspeakers being often presented as &quot;like the Garou, but mages instead of werewolves&quot;. But the single biggest part was one of the game's first sourcebooks, The Book of Chantries, which for one of it's four Tradition chantries had the Dreamspeaker's Lodge of the Grey Squirrel, made up almost entirely of Native Americans/First Nations characters. Even with the frequent mentions of African and Australian Dreamspeakers, and the discussions of other cultures in the original Tradition book, the North Americans face of the Tradition was usually the default stereotype back then. <br /> <br /> As for why the Tradition exists in-game, Naioba and Star of Eagles already had the idea of a grand fraternity/sorority of people bound together by their calling as shaman. But some other people (I'm looking at you, Order of Hermes and Solificati) decided it would be a great place to dump any group that wasn't Eurasian. Some, like the Ngoma and the Kopa Loei, politely told them to pound sand and left. Others, such as the various Mayan factions who joined the Ecstatics, Thanatoics or Verbena, just ignored them and joined who they felt suited them best. But, sadly, the &quot;dumping ground&quot; stigma stuck.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-14T06:03:59-08:00","id":1167586,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1167487#post1167487\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The real reason? Because back in 1993, a number of people working at White Wolf (developers, freelancers, etc.) were really into the whole &quot;Native American as Noble Savage&quot; thing that was big at the time, especially in the wake of Dances With Wolves (as well as drawing on stuff from the 60s and 70s that were retro-cool for Gen-X-ers), and some bought into Marshall Sahlin's &quot;original affluent society&quot; theories about primitive hunter gatherers. This was really heavily apparent in 1st ed Werewolf stuff, and carried over into 1st and 2nd ed Mage, with the Dreamspeakers being often presented as &quot;like the Garou, but mages instead of werewolves&quot;. But the single biggest part was one of the game's first sourcebooks, The Book of Chantries, which for one of it's four Tradition chantries had the Dreamspeaker's Lodge of the Grey Squirrel, made up almost entirely of Native Americans/First Nations characters. Even with the frequent mentions of African and Australian Dreamspeakers, and the discussions of other cultures in the original Tradition book, the North Americans face of the Tradition was usually the default stereotype back then.<br /> <br /> As for why the Tradition exists in-game, Naioba and Star of Eagles already had the idea of a grand fraternity/sorority of people bound together by their calling as shaman. But some other people (I'm looking at you, Order of Hermes and Solificati) decided it would be a great place to dump any group that wasn't Eurasian. Some, like the Ngoma and the Kopa Loei, politely told them to pound sand and left. Others, such as the various Mayan factions who joined the Ecstatics, Thanatoics or Verbena, just ignored them and joined who they felt suited them best. But, sadly, the &quot;dumping ground&quot; stigma stuck.</div> </div> </div> </div>Those people also specifically tied the Dreamspeakers to African mysticism from pretty much the get-go. Seems like it's more likely that the audience of Mage is as much, if not more responsible. The internet has definitely made information about other mystic/supernatural practices more available to the writers and the hoi polloi than it was back in the day, but &quot;write what (you think) you know&quot; is pretty much the way things work. And people also play what they (think they) know as well. Since most of the people (developers included) weren't anthropologists, it's not an entirely unforgivable sin. Particularly one that a good cultural anthropologist wouldn't forgive them for. Unfortunately, when they tried to correct, like those Mayan groups joining with other Trads in &quot;defiance&quot; of the Eurocentrism of the Big Three, the writers pretty much made a hash of what those groups were all about as well, particularly in the sense that they were integrated parts of an overall whole. (On the flip side of the Eurocentrism keeping groups together BECAUSE they are European, when it seems like they should have broken them up, you don't see Nightshade going &quot;Well, sure, the &lt;insert word for European priestly group of &quot;sacrificers&quot; pertinent to the cultural group involved&gt; are all about death, so let's take out of my club and put them over there with all the other death-obsessed dudes...&quot; Since Death is part of the Cycle when it makes sense to my faux-druid sensibilties, but the concept of dharma is just too weird...)<br /> <br /> But, yes, the somewhat regrettable not-really shamanic mysticism of Werewolf (and I really want to delete the &quot;somewhat&quot;) definitely did cast a long shadow over the Dreamspeakers and not in a way that really benefited their conception or play. .","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-15T11:30:49-08:00","id":1167971,"title":"","content":"Random ideas for Japanese Dreamspeaker characters<br /> <br /> Keepers of the Sacred Fire: Miko shrine maiden who is secretly addicted to dating sim games. <br /> Baruti: Manga artist specializing in historical, dark fantasy and horror stories.<br /> Red Spear Society: Delinquent biker boy martial artist<br /> Spirit Smiths: Apprentice to master swordsmith.<br /> Ghost Wheel Society: Hardcore computer otaku obsessed with finding and identifying new urban and cyber spirits.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-16T08:54:22-08:00","id":1176529,"title":"","content":"As someone who is an actual traditional Native American, I found the Dreamspeakers' books largely insulting. Revised really pissed me off.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-16T09:33:24-08:00","id":1176532,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1176529#post1176529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As someone who is an actual traditional Native American, I found the Dreamspeakers' books largely insulting. Revised really pissed me off.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Totally understandable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1675-weirdboyz","name":"Weirdboyz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"212"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-16T09:56:20-08:00","id":1176536,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1176529#post1176529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As someone who is an actual traditional Native American, I found the Dreamspeakers' books largely insulting. Revised really pissed me off.</div> </div> </div> </div>Could you elaborate on that, please.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-16T11:03:36-08:00","id":1176551,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1176536#post1176536\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Could you elaborate on that, please.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure. They both largely fail at presenting the depth and complexity such a gathering of animist cultures would bring to the table. However the first/second edition book managed to somewhat capture the feeling of a union of Awakened that arose from animist and largely dispossessed cultures, even if it fell into noble-savagery tropes a lot. The Revised book came across as plastic medicine men and newagers that talk to spirits and not shamans of any stripe. And I'm not talking about just from a Native American POV. My group includes Asian members that come from animist cultures there, and they felt the same way. Another player also did hefty hefty research into making an East African Dreamspeaker, which reflected nothing you could find in the books, and made him more disgusted with canon presentations.<br /> <br /> Sure, back in the 90s, there wasn't a lot outside of your local libraries to research on for both the writers and the players. So I can forgive a lot. I mean almost all the Tradition and Convention books failed at their job (1/2E and Revised), but the Dreamspeakers really suffered. Other traditions became better fleshed out both historically and diversely in their internal factions. I'm guessing Revised decided to try and get away from stereotypes by heavily white washing the Speakers and shoving various groups that could have reflected a more nuanced organization into other Traditions or made them crafts. It's a common thing when majority cultures try to present outsiders. They either stereotype and romanticize us too much or try so hard not to, it erases our very presence as a distinct people.<br /> <br /> Also there's no presentation of how the hell any of this is organized beyond some thin ideological camps, a generic and redundant subgroup, and the meeting in dreams (which I don't think carried over to Revised). If memory serves me correct just about every other Tradition became filled with numerous sub-groups and factions even if the overall tradition was not a coherent organization as a whole. The Dreamspeakers, out of all the traditions, should have had numerous sub-groups that were almost Traditions unto themselves, with the ideological camps providing a thin ground for common action. Aside from the founding couple's positive intentions, they were suppose to be the dumping ground for all those &quot;weirdos with funny skin colors and strange languages&quot; that the more 'civilized' mages didn't want to associate with. I thought this turning of what was initially a very ignorant game design concept into an example of in-game racism was a rather brilliant turn around. Sadly it was wasted.<br /> <br /> I'd like to present a more positive homebrew example, but this post is already getting long.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":21481,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","user":"Onkwe","date":". 01-16-2018, 03:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-16T15:06:26-08:00","id":1176614,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1176551#post1176551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sure. They both largely fail at presenting the depth and complexity such a gathering of animist cultures would bring to the table. However the first/second edition book managed to somewhat capture the feeling of a union of Awakened that arose from animist and largely dispossessed cultures, even if it fell into noble-savagery tropes a lot. The Revised book came across as plastic medicine men and newagers that talk to spirits and not shamans of any stripe. And I'm not talking about just from a Native American POV. My group includes Asian members that come from animist cultures there, and they felt the same way. Another player also did hefty hefty research into making an East African Dreamspeaker, which reflected nothing you could find in the books, and made him more disgusted with canon presentations.<br /> <br /> Sure, back in the 90s, there wasn't a lot outside of your local libraries to research on for both the writers and the players. So I can forgive a lot. I mean almost all the Tradition and Convention books failed at their job (1/2E and Revised), but the Dreamspeakers really suffered. Other traditions became better fleshed out both historically and diversely in their internal factions. I'm guessing Revised decided to try and get away from stereotypes by heavily white washing the Speakers and shoving various groups that could have reflected a more nuanced organization into other Traditions or made them crafts. It's a common thing when majority cultures try to present outsiders. They either stereotype and romanticize us too much or try so hard not to, it erases our very presence as a distinct people.<br /> <br /> Also there's no presentation of how the hell any of this is organized beyond some thin ideological camps, a generic and redundant subgroup, and the meeting in dreams (which I don't think carried over to Revised). If memory serves me correct just about every other Tradition became filled with numerous sub-groups and factions even if the overall tradition was not a coherent organization as a whole. The Dreamspeakers, out of all the traditions, should have had numerous sub-groups that were almost Traditions unto themselves, with the ideological camps providing a thin ground for common action. Aside from the founding couple's positive intentions, they were suppose to be the dumping ground for all those &quot;weirdos with funny skin colors and strange languages&quot; that the more 'civilized' mages didn't want to associate with. I thought this turning of what was initially a very ignorant game design concept into an example of in-game racism was a rather brilliant turn around. Sadly it was wasted.<br /> <br /> I'd like to present a more positive homebrew example, but this post is already getting long.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> &quot;Depth, complexity, plastic, feeling, other players did 'it' better, etc.&quot;<br /> <br /> I'm still a bit confused on the <b>specific details </b>of the books you disliked so strongly. But it might be the amount of caffeine I just consumed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-16T17:55:58-08:00","id":1176640,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted b