pan style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-16T18:34:41-08:00","id":1176656,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fat Larry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1176641#post1176641\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Cool. I'll take your word for it.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It probably didn't help I went off on a rant on the tradition itself rather than focusing on just the books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-16T18:58:02-08:00","id":1176660,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1176656#post1176656\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> It probably didn't help I went off on a rant on the tradition itself rather than focusing on just the books.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It's all good. We all like what we like, and that's perfectly fine. At the same time, we bring a wealth of experiences that our personal to us which helps to personalize our experiences. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">&quot;Steel isn't strong, boy. Flesh is stronger.&quot;</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/95-fat-larry","name":"Fat Larry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar95_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-17T17:02:12-08:00","id":1176860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1176551#post1176551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Also there's no presentation of how the hell any of this is organized beyond some thin ideological camps, a generic and redundant subgroup, and the meeting in dreams (which I don't think carried over to Revised). If memory serves me correct just about every other Tradition became filled with numerous sub-groups and factions even if the overall tradition was not a coherent organization as a whole. The Dreamspeakers, out of all the traditions, should have had numerous sub-groups that were almost Traditions unto themselves, with the ideological camps providing a thin ground for common action.</div> </div> </div> </div>It has struck me recently how relatively few factions there are in the Dreamspeakers; the Revised Tradbook has like six when really there should be dozens.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-17T23:19:44-08:00","id":1176901,"title":"","content":"I think the Dreamspeakers (and well before the Revised book) got screwed because the game set up the idea that a lot of potential Dreamspeakers never joined the Tradition or the Traditions. There's a pretty large number of Crafts that would have been interesting Dreamspeaker factions (a few did join in Revised, though M20 undid most of that), as well as a pretty decent number of animist or shaman oriented groups in other Traditions as factions for them that seem like they'd be more naturally Dreamspeakers (ex: the Akashics have the explicitly shaman Jnani as a faction).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-18T10:15:27-08:00","id":1177002,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1176901#post1176901\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the Dreamspeakers (and well before the Revised book) got screwed because the game set up the idea that a lot of potential Dreamspeakers never joined the Tradition or the Traditions. There's a pretty large number of Crafts that would have been interesting Dreamspeaker factions (a few did join in Revised, though M20 undid most of that), as well as a pretty decent number of animist or shaman oriented groups in other Traditions as factions for them that seem like they'd be more naturally Dreamspeakers (ex: the Akashics have the explicitly shaman Jnani as a faction).</div> </div> </div> </div>The Guide to the Traditions also tried turning 'shamanism' into some sort of weird way to do magick instead of it being the Dreamspeaker's paradigm, similar to technomagick. So I'm not sure if they just didn't know what to do with them or were trying to downplay the group since they felt it was flawed and racist down to the root of its concept, but didn't want to alienate parts of the fanbase by eliminating them. Even with the later approach of placing shamanistic/animistic factions in other groups, this was also kind of insulting, since animist peoples couldn't be themselves and existed as exotic offshoots of other Traditions' (and largely non-animist European) paradigms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":21481,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","user":"Onkwe","date":". 01-18-2018, 01:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-18T14:11:47-08:00","id":1177046,"title":"","content":"Dead Magic 2 has some good material for European shamans, and it says several times in several books that the Hermetics tried clumping several groups (including the Ngoma and the Kopa Loei) into the Dreamspeakers. Which insulted a few of them pretty thoroughly. But of you're asking for non-Native American shamanistic practices.... Do some research. Do a lot of research. Mongols, Native Americans, and a whole bunch of groups got put together into the Dreamspeakers. In a way, they're the Tradition that started off with the least coherent identity. Most of the group didn't even have a single language to communicate. Which means that yes, Shinto practitioners ended up lumped in with Contraries and other native American medicine men, as well as Mongolian shamans, along with anyone else that primarily talked to spirits, but didn't fit neatly into the Verbena, Akashics, or Euthanatos. Part of the reason they have problems going into detail on the Dreamspeakers is that there's so much detail to go into, and they have limited space.<br /> <br /> Does it seem like the Native Americans are running things for the Tradition? It's possible, because they've had traditions to help lay the basis for dealing with people who don't speak the language. And frankly, if your chronicle is taking place mostly in North America, that's the sort of Dreamspeaker you're most likely to run into. Head to Outer Mongolia, and you're not going to see a Native American medicine man unless he's dropping by to chat with a colleague. Shamanistic practices tend to be very tied to the people. So unless the people are there, they tend not to be highly mobile.<br /> <br /> Also, different tribe in North America have different practices. A Cherokee medicine man doesn't operate like a Lakota, who doesn't operate like an Osage, who doesn't operate like an Apache and so on. Again, space concerns in the books as they learned more about what they were writing about. Of course, I'm the jerk who went urban primitive when he played a Dreamspeaker, not Native American.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23716,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23716-scottish","user":"Scottish","date":". 01-18-2018, 05:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23716-scottish","name":"Scottish","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23716_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-18T15:14:35-08:00","id":1177063,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1177002#post1177002\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The Guide to the Traditions also tried turning 'shamanism' into some sort of weird way to do magick instead of it being the Dreamspeaker's paradigm, similar to technomagick. So I'm not sure if they just didn't know what to do with them or were trying to downplay the group since they felt it was flawed and racist down to the root of its concept, but didn't want to alienate parts of the fanbase by eliminating them. Even with the later approach of placing shamanistic/animistic factions in other groups, this was also kind of insulting, since animist peoples couldn't be themselves and existed as exotic offshoots of other Traditions' (and largely non-animist European) paradigms.</div> </div> </div> </div>My personal preference is trying to make the Traditions more about magical styles/paradigms, and not about cultures. Otherwise things start getting really racist. Like, there are times when the Akashics become the tradition of people with epicanthic folds, and that makes me super-uncomfortable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-18T15:21:42-08:00","id":1177068,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1177002#post1177002\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Guide to the Traditions also tried turning 'shamanism' into some sort of weird way to do magick instead of it being the Dreamspeaker's paradigm, similar to technomagick.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again though, this predates the books you're assigning them to. I'm not trying to say your general reaction is incorrect, but mostly that the books are dealing with issues from previous editions.<br /> <br /> In late 2e, there was a supplement called Spirit Ways, which was a book about shaman mages and explicitly not just about Dreamspeakers. So this was something the game was already doing, along with shaman Crafts not being Dreamspeakers.<br /> <br /> That's sort of the problem. Dreamspeakers have never had a lock on shamanism in the setting. Not all shamans in Mage are Dreamspeakers, and not all Dreamspeakers are even properly assigned as shaman (despite how broad that term is). Animism is certainly an essential common factor for all Dreamspeakers, but not all Dreamspeakers interact with spirits in a fashion that's specifically in the role of a shaman (though this gets complicated because defining shamanism isn't exactly easy); especially any of the more syncretistic style Dreamspeakers.<br /> <br /> In M20 terms (because they do make this easier to discuss) Shamanism isn't a Paradigm, it's a Practice. It's a method of magic rather than a core set of beliefs about how the universe works in general.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even with the later approach of placing shamanistic/animistic factions in other groups, this was also kind of insulting, since animist peoples couldn't be themselves and existed as exotic offshoots of other Traditions' (and largely non-animist European) paradigms. </div> </div> </div>I don't think this is accurate. Not that what I think is accurate is any better...<br /> <br /> Most of the &quot;animist/shaman factions in other Traditions,&quot; are the non-Eurocentric Traditions. To make the Akashics the &quot;Asian mages,&quot; they have Asian shamans and animists. The Euthanatos get it - some what validly since there are shamanistic practices that focus more on human spirits than general animism - to make sure they get all the &quot;deal with ghost&quot; practices. The Cult of Ecstasy get it because of the very common association of inducing trance states as magical practice.<br /> <br /> The Verbena are the only European-centric Tradition that got a lot of &quot;exotic offshoot&quot; attitude with their shaman factions, but it's largely an artifact of Mage largely ignoring European shamanism until Dead Mage 2 (as noted above) as something to build into the Verbena from the start.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-30T16:03:13-08:00","id":1180919,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1177068#post1177068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Again though, this predates the books you're assigning them to. I'm not trying to say your general reaction is incorrect, but mostly that the books are dealing with issues from previous editions.<br /> <br /> In late 2e, there was a supplement called Spirit Ways, which was a book about shaman mages and explicitly not just about Dreamspeakers. So this was something the game was already doing, along with shaman Crafts not being Dreamspeakers.<br /> <br /> That's sort of the problem. Dreamspeakers have never had a lock on shamanism in the setting. Not all shamans in Mage are Dreamspeakers, and not all Dreamspeakers are even properly assigned as shaman (despite how broad that term is). Animism is certainly an essential common factor for all Dreamspeakers, but not all Dreamspeakers interact with spirits in a fashion that's specifically in the role of a shaman (though this gets complicated because defining shamanism isn't exactly easy); especially any of the more syncretistic style Dreamspeakers..</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1177068#post1177068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't think this is accurate. Not that what I think is accurate is any better...<br /> <br /> Most of the &quot;animist/shaman factions in other Traditions,&quot; are the non-Eurocentric Traditions. To make the Akashics the &quot;Asian mages,&quot; they have Asian shamans and animists. The Euthanatos get it - some what validly since there are shamanistic practices that focus more on human spirits than general animism - to make sure they get all the &quot;deal with ghost&quot; practices. The Cult of Ecstasy get it because of the very common association of inducing trance states as magical practice.<br /> <br /> The Verbena are the only European-centric Tradition that got a lot of &quot;exotic offshoot&quot; attitude with their shaman factions, but it's largely an artifact of Mage largely ignoring European shamanism until Dead Mage 2 (as noted above) as something to build into the Verbena from the start.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry I haven't responded in a while, I just didn't have time to sit and write out a proper response. I poorly worded what I meant in my last post. I am aware of Spirit Ways and largely liked that book, including how they described how not all shamans and animist people needed to be Dreamspeakers. My complaint is how how the writers failed to expand on the Tradition, and kept partitioning factions that could have highlighted the hodgepodge nature of the Dreamspeakers into other Traditions or into Crafts. Like they could have included non-Shamanistic types into the Dreamspeakers to expand on the theme of it being a dumping ground (imagine the Nagoma as a major faction of the Dreamspeakers, or the Mesoamerican groups they described going to the Euthantos and Cultists). This could have been contrasted by how the Sami and other similar European cultures were welcomed into the the Verbena or the Janni being apart of the Akashics to drive this point home that the Traditions can be more than 'gamey' archetype casting styles.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1177068#post1177068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In M20 terms (because they do make this easier to discuss) Shamanism isn't a Paradigm, it's a Practice. It's a method of magic rather than a core set of beliefs about how the universe works in general.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm largely not a fan of most of M20's sample paradigms and their separation of it from practices. Most were not paradigms IMO, but attitudes about life and/or redundant of other categories.<br /> <br /> <br /> A side note, sorry if I came off as an ass who 'knows better than the writers'. I've been hanging around too many people lately who feel they're the unappreciated geniuses of White Wolf/Onyx Path properties. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":21481,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","user":"Onkwe","date":". 01-30-2018, 07:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-31T01:44:30-08:00","id":1181045,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1180919#post1180919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm largely not a fan of most of M20's sample paradigms and their separation of it from practices. Most were not paradigms IMO, but attitudes about life and/or redundant of other categories.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see M20's approach as a problem, but rather as a feature of its modular design.<br /> <br /> Paradigms, in M20, are not simply attitudes about life, but rather statements describing base assumptions about Reality, as the mage sees it. It's the foundation of their particular worldview. Note that when I say &quot;base assumptions&quot; and &quot;foundation&quot;, I am being deliberate. The Paradigms, as described in M20, are not meant to be taken as-is. Players are meant to build upon them as frameworks. M20 needed to be generalized for the sake of both brevity AND the need to be applicable to as wide a swath of potential worldviews a mage could have.<br /> <br /> The Focus, however, is in the details, as it were. Players are meant to take the Paradigm examples and flesh them out themselves. Sure, we could SAY a mage believes that Creation Is Divine And Alive, but what does that MEAN to an individual mage? While the descriptions in the book are generalized, a mage character's individual Paradigm is meant to be more specific, describing their particular view of Reality. In the aforementioned Divine And Alive Creation, such a Paradigm means different things to different mages. A Dreamspeaker may see it as every part of the world having its own spirit, a Verbana could see it as the will of Mother Earth, a Hindu Euthanatos or Chorister could see it pantheistically as the world being part of Brahmin. Even a Progenitor could see a Divine And Alive Creation in the Earth's biosphere.<br /> <br /> All of these are similar, yet distinct, views on what Reality is, and those distinctions color how a mage goes about performing Magick. They also take into account Practice, just as that Practice must, by necessity, be informed by the Paradigm. While M20 breaks a mage's magick into Paradigm and Practice, this is for the sake of simplicity in explaining abstract philosophical ideas. In truth, though, a mage's Focus must, in the end, be examined holistically. Paradigm, Practice, and Instruments must all come together to describe what the mage does, how they do it, and why they believe they can do it that way.<br /> <br /> Why does one Dreamspeaker make offerings and chant songs? Because it placates the spirits. And why is he trying to placate the spirits? Because he believes that the world is full of spirits, and that those spirits underpin all of Reality. Instruments are dictated by Practice, which is informed by Paradigm. Divisions between these categories is for the benefit of players just trying to wrap their heads around complicated and abstract concepts. For the mage herself, each is connected and intuitive, each step following &quot;obviously&quot; from one to the next.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-31T16:19:50-08:00","id":1181265,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1181045#post1181045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't see M20's approach as a problem, but rather as a feature of its modular design.<br /> <br /> Paradigms, in M20, are not simply attitudes about life, but rather statements describing base assumptions about Reality, as the mage sees it. It's the foundation of their particular worldview. Note that when I say &quot;base assumptions&quot; and &quot;foundation&quot;, I am being deliberate. The Paradigms, as described in M20, are not meant to be taken as-is. Players are meant to build upon them as frameworks. M20 needed to be generalized for the sake of both brevity AND the need to be applicable to as wide a swath of potential worldviews a mage could have.<br /> <br /> The Focus, however, is in the details, as it were. Players are meant to take the Paradigm examples and flesh them out themselves. Sure, we could SAY a mage believes that Creation Is Divine And Alive, but what does that MEAN to an individual mage? While the descriptions in the book are generalized, a mage character's individual Paradigm is meant to be more specific, describing their particular view of Reality. In the aforementioned Divine And Alive Creation, such a Paradigm means different things to different mages. A Dreamspeaker may see it as every part of the world having its own spirit, a Verbana could see it as the will of Mother Earth, a Hindu Euthanatos or Chorister could see it pantheistically as the world being part of Brahmin. Even a Progenitor could see a Divine And Alive Creation in the Earth's biosphere.<br /> <br /> All of these are similar, yet distinct, views on what Reality is, and those distinctions color how a mage goes about performing Magick. They also take into account Practice, just as that Practice must, by necessity, be informed by the Paradigm. While M20 breaks a mage's magick into Paradigm and Practice, this is for the sake of simplicity in explaining abstract philosophical ideas. In truth, though, a mage's Focus must, in the end, be examined holistically. Paradigm, Practice, and Instruments must all come together to describe what the mage does, how they do it, and why they believe they can do it that way.<br /> <br /> Why does one Dreamspeaker make offerings and chant songs? Because it placates the spirits. And why is he trying to placate the spirits? Because he believes that the world is full of spirits, and that those spirits underpin all of Reality. Instruments are dictated by Practice, which is informed by Paradigm. Divisions between these categories is for the benefit of players just trying to wrap their heads around complicated and abstract concepts. For the mage herself, each is connected and intuitive, each step following &quot;obviously&quot; from one to the next.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I do like the shift for Mages to be more individuals in regards to how they approach paradigm, and I can see how it's very helpful approached more holistically after your post. We just got use to doing heavy research on our own to develop our character's paradigms. I also tend to prefer more discarding foci for a sphere than disregarding the tool. We had a few characters that had very few foci, but they were heavily used in creative ways.<br /> <br /> Anyways, sorry to keep hijacking the thread for this. Feel free to move on everyone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":21481,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","user":"Onkwe","date":". 01-31-2018, 07:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe","name":"Onkwe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar21481_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"162"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-31T20:02:17-08:00","id":1181317,"title":"","content":"Just pointing this out: M20's Focus/Paradigm/Practice/Instruments setup isn't really anything new: every one of those four things existed in both 2e and Rev. What changed in M20 is that the terms were shuffled around, and the various components were packaged differently:<br /> <br /> 1. M20's Focus is what the earlier editions called \u201cParadigm\u201d. It's an umbrella term for the various components and how they tie together. Meaningless on its own, but of highest importance once you've synthesized its components.<br /> <br /> 2. M20's Paradigm is what the earlier editions called Philosophy. You'll find the M2e/MRev Philosophies embedded in the Tradition write-ups, whereas their M20 counterpart gets its own section, with the group write-ups referencing it. <br /> <br /> 3. M20's Practice is M2e's Style and MRev's Theory &amp; Practice. Again, you'll find the 2e and Rev versions embedded in the Tradition write-ups, while the M20 iteration is standalone. As well, the M20 version is where the various parts of Focus get tied together, through the embedded Associated Paradigms/Abilities/Instruments lists. It also references Practices in the group write-ups, but leaves it to the reader to look up the details.<br /> <br /> 4. M20's Instruments are M2e's and MRev's Foci. They're the only part of this that have always had their own section for write-ups. Earlier editions embedded lists of these in the Tradition write-ups; M20 assumes that the reader will reference the lists embedded in the Practice write-ups.<br /> <br /> It really isn't anything new; just a repackaging that lets M20's Focus stand somewhat independent of the metaphysical groups of the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-31T20:13:23-08:00","id":1181321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Onkwe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1167121-not-native-american-dreamspeakers?p=1176551#post1176551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd like to present a more positive homebrew example, but this post is already getting long.</div> </div> </div> </div>I for one would love to see said 